                        icon: "AlertTriangle",
                        content: "Honesty cuts both ways. Pattaya is the wrong choice if:\n\n**You want a beautiful beach.** This is the biggest single caveat. **Pattaya Beach itself is mediocre** — narrow, busy, with water that isn't what you're picturing when you imagine Thailand. Jomtien is considerably better, and Koh Larn's beaches are genuinely lovely, but if postcard sand is the primary goal, **Krabi, Koh Lanta, Koh Samui or the Andaman coast will serve you far better**. People who arrive expecting Phuket-quality beach and get Pattaya Beach are the single largest group of disappointed visitors.\n\n**You want unspoiled or \"authentic\" Thailand.** Pattaya is developed, commercial and unapologetic about it. High-rise condos line the bay. If you're looking for a fishing village atmosphere, this isn't it — though Bang Saray, 20 minutes south, still partly is.\n\n**You're bothered by the nightlife industry's existence.** Even if you avoid the districts entirely, Pattaya's economy is visibly shaped by it, and there's a persistent open presence of it in parts of the city. Some visitors find that uncomfortable regardless of where they stay, and that's a legitimate reason to choose elsewhere.\n\n**You're travelling solo as a young woman and want a relaxed beach town.** You'll be fine and it's safe, but the atmosphere in central and south Pattaya can be wearing. Jomtien or Naklua fix most of it; Krabi or Koh Lanta fix all of it.\n\n**You have limited time in Thailand overall.** If you have ten days total and haven't seen Chiang Mai or the Andaman islands, Pattaya shouldn't be on the list. It earns its place on a second trip, or as a short add-on to Bangkok — not as a headline destination.",
